\section{Mathematical Background}{
A \strong{Galois Field} (or \strong{finite field}) \eqn{GF(q)} is an algebraic structure
containing exactly \eqn{q} elements with well-defined addition, subtraction,
multiplication, and division operations satisfying the field axioms.
\subsection{Fundamental Theorem}{

Finite fields exist if and only if the order \eqn{q} is a \strong{prime power}:
\deqn{q = p^n}
where \eqn{p} is a prime number (the \strong{characteristic}) and \eqn{n \geq 1}
is a positive integer (the \strong{extension degree}).
}

\subsection{Why Galois Fields for Group Testing?}{

The Polynomial Pools algorithm constructs pooling matrices by evaluating
polynomials over \eqn{GF(q)}. The field structure ensures:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Closure}: All operations remain within the field
\item \strong{Invertibility}: Every non-zero element has a multiplicative inverse
\item \strong{Polynomial roots}: Polynomials of degree \eqn{d} have at most \eqn{d} roots
}

These properties guarantee the \strong{intersection bound}: any two samples share
at most \eqn{d-1} pools, which is essential for the detection guarantees.
}

\subsection{Relationship to Polynomial Pools and P-BEST}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{Galois Field}: Provides the arithmetic foundation
\item \strong{Polynomial Pools}: Uses GF(q) to construct pooling matrices
\item \strong{P-BEST}: A specific PP design using GF(8) = GF(2^3)
}
}
}

